      "fieldData": {
       "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Approval Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is the workflow for document approval?\", \"a\": \"A document approval workflow is the sequence of reviews, checks, and sign-offs a document passes through before it counts as final. An author submits a draft, named approvers review it, and the approved version is released. Routing can be serial, parallel, or conditional: one approver after another, several at once, or a route picked by the document's details.\"}, {\"q\": \"How to design document approval workflows with exception handling?\", \"a\": \"Map the normal route first, then add one rule for each way it breaks: no answer, a rejection, an edit after approval, or an approver who leaves the company. Give each rule an owner and a deadline. In Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-automation-builder'>AI automation builder</a>, you write those rules in plain English, and Emergent builds the app that runs them, alerts included.\"}, {\"q\": \"How AI agents make decisions in document approval workflows?\", \"a\": \"They should not make the final call: an AI agent reads and sorts, and your written rules and a named approver decide. The agent pulls out the document's type and client, and the rules pick the route from those values. Save what the agent read next to each approval for anyone checking it later. Apps built with Emergent can include that AI reading step.\"}, {\"q\": \"How organizations automate document approvals workflows?\", \"a\": \"They move the route out of email and into software. Uploading a document starts the workflow, rules pick the approvers, reminders replace follow-up emails, and each decision is stored against the version it covers. Producing the draft in the first place, by filling a template from a record, is <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-automation-builder/document'>document automation</a>, and it can hand each new draft straight to the approval route.\"}, {\"q\": \"How AI reduces bottlenecks in automated document approval workflows?\", \"a\": \"AI cuts the wait before an approver ever sees the document. It reads the type, the client, and the amount on upload, so the rules route each file at once instead of after someone sorts it by hand. The decision stays with a person, and a reminder goes out when a step runs past its deadline.\"}, {\"q\": \"How to automate document approval workflows in my business?\", \"a\": \"Automate it around one owner: a person who knows every route and can change a rule the day a policy changes. Keep the written rules where staff can read them, and check the average wait per document type each month. In Emergent, that owner changes a rule by describing the new one in a prompt.\"}, {\"q\": \"How to set up document approval workflows?\", \"a\": \"Set one up per document type by deciding four things: what starts it, who approves and in what order, how long each step may take, and which version an approval covers. Write those down before you build anything. Purchase orders add one more input, an amount that picks the approvers, and <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-automation-builder/purchase-order'>purchase order automation</a> covers that case.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should a document approval workflow system record?\", \"a\": \"It should record four details for every decision: who decided, which version they saw, when, and any comment. Keep rejected versions as well as approved ones. That record answers the question a client or an auditor asks months later: who approved this exact text, and when.\"}, {\"q\": \"Do I need dedicated document approval workflow software?\", \"a\": \"No, not for a handful of documents a month: a shared folder with a checklist can hold up. Once you need reminders, delegates, or a record per version, use software. Check whether each tool charges for people who only approve. Emergent has no per-approver seats.\"}, {\"q\": \"How does an approval workflow fit into document management?\", \"a\": \"It is the part that decides when a stored draft becomes the approved version. Document management is a system or process used to capture, track, and store electronic documents; the approval workflow adds who must approve it and by when. Let only the latest draft enter the route, and keep every earlier version on file.\"}]}; </script></div></div>",
       "alpb---prompt": "<p>Build a document approval workflow that runs on its own and logs every step.</p><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbPrompt = {\"default\": \"Build a document approval workflow that runs on its own and logs every step.\", \"chips\": [{\"label\": \"Approval Order\", \"add\": \"Add an approval order that sends each document to one approver after another.\"}, {\"label\": \"Parallel Review\", \"add\": \"Add parallel review that sends one document to several approvers at the same time.\"}, {\"label\": \"Version Locking\", \"add\": \"Add version locking that clears an approval when someone edits the approved document.\"}, {\"label\": \"Late Reminders\", \"add\": \"Add late reminders that nudge an approver and pass a stalled document to a delegate.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
      }
